---
scope: src/service/install/provisionBrain
description: "Provisions the brain layer at install time by scaffolding the user-owned brain config from the bundled template and planning the brain MCP entry derived from it."
intent: >
  provisionBrain is the install category that stands up the brain layer
  on a fresh host. It scaffolds the brain config file from the bundled
  template into the user-owned configuration directory, leaving every
  unwired slot as the structural unwired-slot signal so the user can
  finish wiring later. It then plans the brain MCP server entry strictly
  from the scaffolded brain config, never from a launcher constant. The
  bundled template is the default way to satisfy the required-section
  contract and the latest source a repair draws missing sections from.
  The category also self-heals whenever the brain config is missing.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - The brain config is scaffolded from the bundled template at first install, with unwired slots left as the structural unwired-slot signal so the user finishes wiring after install. The default template targets Obsidian, and a user-authored config for any other backend that satisfies the required-section contract is equally valid.
    - "Brain config idempotency is presence-only: once the file lands inside the user-owned configuration directory the user owns it forever, customizations are never proposed for overwrite, and a missing file self-heals on the next call."
    - The brain MCP entry's prescription is always derived from the scaffolded brain config — its launcher shape, its arguments, and any unwired slots flow from the config bytes through the parser and interpolator unchanged.
    - This category plans only the brain config scaffold and the brain MCP entry. Brain entry-point artifacts and the brain root itself are seeded later by the integration's own prose, not by this category.
    - The bundled template satisfies the current required-section contract in full, so a fresh scaffold boots clean. Its kanban seeds plant an empty board and a kanban working guide into the brain in backend-agnostic language, and its orientation points agents at the board.
    - The brain configuration command the template teaches creates a missing brain config after asking which backend — the bundled template for Obsidian, agent-written wiring plus user-confirmed generic sections for any other — and repairs an outdated one by adding only the missing required sections. Agents read the published contract and the missing-section blocks from the latest template through the install action's read-only brain step, then seed brain-side files for new seed sections skip-on-exists and validate the result against the real parser before finishing.
  undesired:
    - This category emitting a step that writes any brain entry-point artifact, creates the brain root directory, or otherwise lands user-storage content. Those surfaces require slot semantics this category is forbidden from knowing.
    - Any code path here that introspects which argument slot in the brain config means what — by index, by sentinel string, by token-shape matcher, or by frontmatter field declaring slot kinds. Slot semantics belong to the integration's prose, never to this package.
    - "A would-overwrite step returned for any path inside the user-owned configuration directory under any rationale, including landing newer template defaults. The directory is the ownership boundary."
    - Constructing the brain MCP entry inline from a launcher constant, hardcoded command, or hardcoded argument list anywhere in this category. The scaffolded brain config is the single source of truth for launcher bytes.
    - Substituting the structural unwired-slot signal for an empty string, a literal placeholder, a fallback path, or a dropped array element anywhere along the plan path. The unwired-slot signal must reach the MCP entry prescription verbatim so the downstream write surface can refuse to land a partially-wired entry.
    - A repair that alters an existing section, the launcher wiring, or any user customization, or that draws missing sections from anywhere but the latest bundled template. Repair adds what is missing and nothing else, and the runtime brain tool never returns seed bytes.
---
